Central Contract — Mission Definition Pipeline

Every stage above writes to one formal artifact, the Mission Definition, which
the Planning Core converts into a Mission Package for Digital Twin execution:

Field → Preparation → Mission Designer → Mission Definition → Planning Core →
Mission Package → Mission Library → Digital Twin → Mission Control

The UI never connects planning actions directly to execution; it only edits the
Mission Definition.

Revised Subdivision (authoritative)

Phase 10D is now subdivided as follows. Sub-phases 10D.3–10D.7 only enrich the
Mission Definition contract established in 10D.2 — they add no new execution path.

| Sub-phase | Name | Enriches the Mission Definition with |
|-----------|------|--------------------------------------|
| 10D.1 | Architectural Analysis | (analysis only — completed) |
| 10D.2 | **Mission Definition Pipeline** | the contract itself: persistence, Planning Core integration, Mission Package generation, fleet inventory, field persistence |
| 10D.3 | Field Acquisition | field geometry, images, zones, obstacles, environmental information |
| 10D.4 | Mission Designer | crop zones, operation type, route preferences, manual editing, automatic planning request |
| 10D.5 | Fleet Configuration | available drones, capabilities, tank/product information, operational constraints |
| 10D.6 | Mission Review | validates generated plan, resource requirements, estimated execution, Digital Twin deployment readiness |
| 10D.7 | Mission Library | mission storage, reusable templates, scheduling metadata, historical reference |

Mission execution is not a separately-built module: Mission Review (10D.6)
deploys the Mission Package to the **existing** Mission Control, which remains
the execution visualization layer. The new pipeline feeds Mission Control; it
does not replace it.

